<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khitans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khitan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khitans were a nomadic people of the Mongolian and Manchurian steppe who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the tenth century. Earlier they had been among the peoples subject to or neighbouring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>. The Khitan Liao state was a major rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, which paid it annual tribute to maintain peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khitans.jpg" alt="Khitans" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hu Gui · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MongolHuntersSong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under his son Muqan Qaghan (553–572), expansion was extraordinary: the remaining Rouran were crushed, the <b>Khitans</b> defeated in the east, and Bumin's brother Istämi, ruling the western wing, destroyed the Hephthalites in alliance with Sasanian Persia and pushed towards the Caspian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qara Khitai was the western remnant of the Liao dynasty of the <b>Khitans</b>, a Sinicized Para-Mongolic people of eastern Mongolia and north-east China, whose empire the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty conquered between 1114 and 1125.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kyrgyz took little interest in the empire they had destroyed, leaving Kulug Bagha in charge of the Orkhon Valley, which the <b>Khitans</b> seized in 890; other Uyghurs went south to Ganzhou and Qocho, or to the Karluks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The third category, the Han ren, took in the subjects of the former Jin state in the north: northern Chinese, together with <b>Khitans</b>, Jurchens and Koreans.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li><li><blockquote>Abaoji, the Khitan leader later known as Emperor Taizu of Liao, became khagan of the <b>Khitans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
